  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B048",
        "laureates": [
          "Antoine Georges",
          "Dieter Vollhardt",
          "Gabriel Kotliar"
        ],
        "citation": "for dynamical mean-field theory of strongly correlated electrons"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B005",
    "B019"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I now favor dynamical mean-field theory because it isolates a simple, general mechanism for treating local electronic correlations while making a previously intractable class of materials quantitatively accessible. The construction is mature, has become a common language across correlated-electron physics, and has repeatedly been confronted productively with experiment. Vollhardt's infinite-coordination limit and the Georges-Kotliar formulation provide a coherent core attribution.",
    "case_against": "DMFT is not exact for finite-dimensional materials, and its development was sufficiently multi-stage that the division of foundational credit among the proposed three requires documentary scrutiny. A prize citation must recognize the theory itself rather than imply that it alone gives a complete description of every correlated material.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson stresses that DMFT transformed correlated-electron theory, is widely benchmarked, and has strong links to spectroscopy and materials experiments, while noting the difficulty of apportioning credit.",
        "response": "I agree, and this experimental contact strengthens the case beyond methodological elegance. I would nevertheless make the citation narrower than a claim to solve correlated materials generally: the decisive achievement is the dynamical mean-field framework and its controlled organizing principle."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth favors optical-lattice quantum simulation for its unusually direct control of interacting many-body systems, while emphasizing that its foundational credit extends beyond three people.",
        "response": "The atomic platform is exceptionally powerful and remains a serious alternative, but its prize-sized discovery encompasses several developments and makes selective attribution especially difficult. DMFT has a more sharply identifiable theoretical core and a longer, more unified record of consequences across correlated-electron physics."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that circuit QED has a clear theory-experiment attribution and a mature, reproducible physical platform.",
        "response": "That is a persuasive case for B007, particularly its clean link between Blais's framework and the Wallraff-Schoelkopf experiments. I give B048 priority because it supplied a broadly transferable statistical and many-body framework whose scientific consequences are already independent of any one technological trajectory."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review supports the proposed three-person division of credit for the successive formulation and development of DMFT.",
      "How narrowly the citation should delimit single-site DMFT from its cluster and materials-specific descendants."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict with the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. I share the Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but no specific collaboration is indicated."
  }
}
